          "urls": [
            {
              "description": "According to the embodied cognition­ theory, which claims that concepts’ representation is grounded in sensory and motor components, abstract concepts are grounded in interoception, which is processed in the Anterior Insula (AIns). However, it is not clear whether interoception and abstract concepts share common anatomical substrates, and if yes, whether AIns is one candidate. In this study, we used repetitive Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation (rTMS) on healthy human participants (N=25, 19 females) to examine whether left and right AIns play a role in both abstract concepts and interoception. The heartbeat counting task served as a measure of interoceptive accuracy, and the semantic similarity judgment of semantic performance. Concepts were characterized according to both a categorical approach, contrasting three categories of concepts, namely social and emotion (abstract categories), and objects (concrete category), and a dimensional approach, collecting semantic ratings on the emotio",
